/tmp/paper_1504_src/vorseps.tex:5:Armed with a good understanding of Voronoi diagrams and knowledge about the existence of short balanced nooses in plane graphs, we can combine these two ingredients to design a Divide\&Conquer algorithm for \covProb. More precisely, if $\SolObj$ is the optimum solution to the considered instance of \covProb, then the algorithm will iterate through all the possible candidates for balanced nooses of the Voronoi diagram $\dgm_\SolObj$. Each candidate noose will separate the instance into a number of subinstances, which will be solved recursively. For the correct selection of the balanced noose of $\dgm_\SolObj$, each of the subinstances will contain only at most $\frac{2}{3}k$ objects from the solution, and hence we will apply the algorithm recursively only to instances with at most this value of the parameter; This will ensure that the running time is as promised in Theorem~\ref{sec:algo}.
