The paper is “Subexponential parameterized algorithms for planar and geometric facility location problems.” Its headline result is itself an exact \(n^{O(\sqrt{k})}\) algorithm, so an unqualified Track A claim would be wrong. I’m now checking whether the paper’s native disk/planar-object problems support a genuine Track B construction: a costly standard route at shipping scale, but a short structural route, while avoiding the fixed-radius/bidimensional easy regimes the paper calls out.
