### 9 hard google/gemini-3.8-flash 397537178 solved None
To cover all clients $T(P_j)$, affine invariance allows us to work entirely in the original coordinates. The parabolic points $P_j = (jL, j^2)$ lie on a strictly convex curve, so any point $P_j$ on the lower boundary not explicitly chosen as a vertex lies strictly outside the convex hull. 

Thus, a polygon $C(i, x_i)$ covers only its specified vertex clients: $G_i$, all $B_{i, z}$ for $z \neq x_i$, and the predecessor point $B_{p(i), g_{p(i)}(x_i)}$. To ensure that every client is covered when selecting one polygon $C(i, x_i)$ for each group $i \in \{0, \dots, 17\}$, the missing client $B_{i, x_i}$ must be covered by the successor polygon $C(s(i), x_{s(i)})$, which includes $B_{i, g_i(x_{s(i)})}$. Hence, we require:
$$x_i = g_i(x_{s(i)}) \pmod{4001}$$

Each permutation $g_i$ is an affine map $g_i(x) \equiv a_i x + b_i \pmod{4001}$. Composing the permutations along the three disjoint 6-cycles gives a unique fixed point for each cycle, from which all $x_i$ are uniquely determined:

