To cover all transformed parabola points $T(P_j)$, we observe that the invertible affine transformation $T$ preserves convexity and extreme points. Since the points $P_j$ lie on the strictly convex curve $y = x^2/L^2$, each point $P_j$ can only be covered by a polygon if it is chosen as one of that polygon's vertices.

Each polygon $C(i, x_i)$ covers the apex, endpoints, $G_i$, all points $B_{i, z}$ except for $z = x_i$, and precisely one point from group $p(i)$, namely $B_{p(i), g_{p(i)}(x_i)}$. By selecting one polygon from each group $i \in \{0, \dots, 17\}$, every $G_i$ is covered. For the points $B_{i, z}$, each polygon $C(i, x_i)$ leaves exactly one point $B_{i, x_i}$ uncovered. The only other polygon capable of covering a point in group $i$ is $C(s(i), x_{s(i)})$, which covers $B_{i, g_i(x_{s(i)})}$. Hence, we must have:
$$x_i = g_i(x_{s(i)})$$
for each group $i$, which decouples into three independent systems of equations along the three 6-cycles of the successor permutation $s$. 

