-affine recognition. These instances are easy with code—the reference algorithm
-is the reason for Track B—and must not be described as distributional
-NP-hardness.
-The four failed attacks were equal-size outlier selection, start-zero greedy
-propagation, eight structure-aware random propagated starts, and
-constant/index ansatzes. A generic ILP/LP package was not run because the deliverable is standard-library
-only; the distribution-specific propagation algorithm is strictly more direct.
-The canonical key normalizes declaration order and global affine coordinate
-changes, but does not solve arbitrary incidence-graph isomorphism. Finally,
-the polygons are represented exactly by vertex-index formulas rather than by
-materializing roughly 18 million repeated coordinates; `render` fully defines
-those vertices and `verify` performs exact geometric containment.
+The `0/200,000` estimate samples one uniform start per cycle and propagates all
+non-closing equations. It therefore measures the informed `n^3` prior, not the
+naive `n^18` space and not the probability of recognizing ternary digit
+reversal. These instances are easy with code by design; the reference solver is
+why this is Track B. The tested attacks were equal polygon size, zero-start
+greedy propagation, eight random propagated starts, constant/index guesses, and
+fitting affine maps directly to raw table entries. A generic ILP solver was not
+run; exact functional-CSP propagation is more direct for this distribution.
+
+The ternary relabelling may still be recognizable to a strong oracle, and the
+required run is outstanding—this is the principal caveat. The canonical key
+normalizes declaration order and global affine coordinate maps but not arbitrary
+incidence-graph isomorphism. Polygons are specified by exact vertex formulas
+rather than millions of repeated coordinates; `render` defines them fully and
+`verify` performs exact geometric containment.
